WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.200 align:start position:0%
Why do robots suck at using their hands?
When they can break a half marathon

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:11.700 align:start position:0%
record, do a backflip, and okay, maybe
not dance. But really, why not this? A

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.400 align:start position:0%
basic task like picking up an egg is so
difficult for them that people think

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:19.700 align:start position:0%
they're cheating. It's really because
your human hand is insane. Almost a

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:24.800 align:start position:0%
quarter of all the bones in your body
are inside your hands. There are over 30

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.300 align:start position:0%
muscles and 27 joints, giving us over
25° of freedom. A lot of that control

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:33.000 align:start position:0%
starts in the forearm, meaning tendons
have to fit through your narrow wrist

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.800 align:start position:0%
down to each finger. So for a robot,
each joint would need its own motor

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.500 align:start position:0%
squeezed into a pretty small space. But
even if you figure out the right

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:44.400 align:start position:0%
movement, you also need to figure out
the perfect grip strength. Your palm has

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:48.000 align:start position:0%
over 17,000 touch receptors and nerve
endings to register things like pressure

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.700 align:start position:0%
and movement. You don't realize it, but
you're constantly adjusting on the fly

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.400 align:start position:0%
so you don't crush a paper coffee cup or
drop a ball. And that's a lot of sensors

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.600 align:start position:0%
crammed into a tight space with motors
that also need to work together.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:03.300 align:start position:0%
Basically, our hands are engineering
nightmares but biological marvels. But

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.199 align:start position:0%
half your cells are not human. To see
why, subscribe.

